# CFD Verification Studies: Lid-Driven Cavity Flow & Diffuser/Nozzle Analysis

**Course:** AE668 – Computational Aeromechanics and Control of UAVs
**Instructor:** Prof. Navrose, Helicopter and VTOL Lab, Department of Aerospace Engineering, IIT Kanpur
**Type:** Group Project
**Duration:** Jan 2026 – Apr 2026

## Overview

This project performs formal CFD verification on two classical benchmark flow problems — the lid-driven cavity and a compressible diffuser/nozzle — to establish rigorous grid-convergence practices and validate solver accuracy against theoretical results.

## Objectives

- Demonstrate mesh-independence using an ASME-standard verification procedure
- Validate compressible internal flow behavior against 1-D isentropic flow theory
- Build a repeatable verification workflow applicable to future CFD studies

## Methodology

### 1. Grid Convergence Index (GCI) Study
- Conducted on the lid-driven cavity case in ANSYS Fluent
- Four systematically refined meshes were used, ranging from 50×50 to 250×250 cells
- Followed the ASME-standard GCI procedure to quantify discretization error
- Achieved a GCI of less than 1%, confirming mesh-independent results

### 2. Diffuser/Nozzle Flow Validation
- Simulated compressible flow through a diffuser/nozzle geometry across a Mach range of 0.3 to 0.996
- Confirmed the choked-flow condition (M → 1 at the throat) as predicted by 1-D isentropic flow theory
- CFD results matched theoretical predictions within 3–7%

## Key Results

| Study | Result |
|---|---|
| GCI (lid-driven cavity) | < 1% across 4 mesh levels (50×50 → 250×250) |
| Diffuser/nozzle Mach range | 0.3 – 0.996 |
| Agreement with isentropic theory | Within 3–7% |
| Choked flow condition | Confirmed at M → 1 |

## Tools Used
- ANSYS Fluent (compressible solver)
- ANSYS ICEM / meshing tools for structured mesh refinement

